NoMount offline integration and extracted-source tests
=====================================================

From the kernel source root:

    python3 tools/testing/selftests/nomount/test_integration.py
    python3 tools/testing/selftests/nomount/test_integration.py --config out/.config
    python3 tools/testing/selftests/nomount/test_integration.py --host-tests --cc clang
    python3 tools/testing/selftests/nomount/test_integration.py --host-tests \
        --cc 'clang -fsanitize=undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=all'

The integrity-only checks need Python's standard library, not network/compiler.
They check the pinned source, local adaptation hashes, regular files/no nested
Git metadata, parent wiring, default-off/keyring dependency, retained kernel
build flags and resolved built-in configuration. For a disabled output config:

    python3 tools/testing/selftests/nomount/test_integration.py \
        --config /path/to/disabled/.config --expect-disabled

CI invokes the integrity check when the selected defconfig requests NoMount.

--host-tests extracts current nm_key_preparse, UID bloom/add/delete/read helpers,
nm_select_rule and native/compat position-cookie helpers into model.c. Single-
threaded mocks check 44 capability/pointer-size gate cases, 131072 position
roundtrips, 400384 UID/bloom/isolation checks and 1001 rule-selection cases.
Mocks are not real keyring, RCU, VFS, mmap, shutdown or Android operations.

--negative deliberately breaks extracted code in memory. Accepted values:
no-capability-gate, no-rcu-unlock, wrong-compat-cookie, no-uid-bloom.
Each must compile and fail a runtime assertion; a nonzero result is expected.
It does not change repository files. No test configures actual NoMount rules.

See Documentation/filesystems/nomount-integration.md for build/device testing
requirements. These tests do not establish root authorization security, actual
RCU/concurrency safety, filesystem IO correctness or a successful boot.
